---
import CalculatorLayout from '@/layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import TriangleCalculator from '@/components/calculators/TriangleCalculator';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How do I find the area of a triangle from three sides?',
    a: 'Use Heron’s formula. Work out the semi-perimeter s = (a + b + c) ÷ 2, then area = √(s(s − a)(s − b)(s − c)). For sides 7, 8 and 9: s = 12, area = √(12 × 5 × 4 × 3) = √720 ≈ 26.83.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I find the angles of a triangle from its sides?',
    a: 'Use the law of cosines: cos A = (b² + c² − a²) ÷ 2bc. For sides 7, 8 and 9, angle A = 48.19°, B = 58.41° and C = 73.40°, which add up to 180°.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does SSA sometimes give two triangles?',
    a: 'With two sides and an angle that isn’t between them, the third side can sometimes swing into two positions. This is called the ambiguous case. For a = 6, b = 8 and A = 35°, angle B could be 49.89° or 130.11°, so the calculator shows both.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What makes three lengths a valid triangle?',
    a: 'The triangle inequality: each side must be shorter than the other two combined. 3, 4 and 8 can’t make a triangle because 3 + 4 is less than 8.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are the types of triangle?',
    a: 'By sides: equilateral (all equal), isosceles (two equal) and scalene (none equal). By angles: acute (all under 90°), right (one exactly 90°) and obtuse (one over 90°).',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  slug="triangle-calculator"
  title="Triangle Calculator – Sides, Angles & Area (SSS, SAS, ASA)"
  description="Free triangle calculator. Solve any triangle from three sides, two sides and an angle, or two angles and a side: every side, angle, area, perimeter and height."
  intro="Solve any triangle from three sides, two sides and an angle, or two angles and a side, and get every side, angle, the area, perimeter, heights and type."
  faqs={faqs}
  updated="2026-09-27"
>
  <TriangleCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="learn">
    <h2>How to use the triangle calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Pick what you know:</strong> three sides, two sides and an angle, or two angles and a side.</li>
      <li><strong>Enter the values.</strong> Side a is opposite angle A, and so on. Angles are in degrees.</li>
      <li><strong>Read the solved triangle:</strong> a scale diagram, all sides and angles, area, perimeter, heights and radii.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Triangle formulas</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Formula</th><th>Used for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Angle sum</td><td>A + B + C = 180°</td><td>The third angle</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Law of sines</td><td>a ÷ sin A = b ÷ sin B = c ÷ sin C</td><td>ASA, AAS, SSA</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Law of cosines</td><td>c² = a² + b² − 2ab cos C</td><td>SSS, SAS</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Heron’s formula</td><td>√(s(s − a)(s − b)(s − c))</td><td>Area from sides</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Area from SAS</td><td>½ ab sin C</td><td>Area from two sides</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Worked examples</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Given</th><th>Found</th><th>Area</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>a = 7, b = 8, c = 9</td><td>A = 48.19°, B = 58.41°, C = 73.40°</td><td>26.83</td></tr>
        <tr><td>a = 5, b = 7, C = 60°</td><td>c = 6.245, A = 43.90°, B = 76.10°</td><td>15.16</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A = 40°, B = 60°, c = 10</td><td>C = 80°, a = 6.527, b = 8.794</td><td>28.26</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>For right triangles there’s a quicker tool: the <a href="/pythagorean-theorem-calculator">Pythagorean theorem calculator</a>. For other shapes, see the <a href="/area-calculator">area calculator</a>.</p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
